===A3_S5_D1===
Oren: Gather round, gather round once again! #portrait:Oren
Oren: Last night we left the two as they journeyed around our island and the neighbors surrounding, connecting us with ships — not boats. #portrait:Oren
Oren: Elio was a prodigy, after all. In only a week's time, he had expanded their humble ship from a capacity of five to twenty! #portrait:Oren
Narrator: Oren has tapestries with him this time, hung up like banners behind him as he flips in between to illustrate his points.
Noelle: (These look very well-made… are there specialised weavers on Thavmia?) #portrait:NoelleShocked
Oren: As the two learned more of our stories about the Great Disaster, Noah decided that it may be worth reasoning with the kraken! #portrait:Oren
Oren: After all. If creatures were just like you and I, should the Great Disaster not empathise with the loss caused by its floods? #portrait:Oren
Noelle: <i>Hm… but the floods rejuvenate the land, don't they?</i> #portrait:NoelleUm
Amelia: <i>Yeah. It's pretty much common knowledge that it happens for a reason.</i> #portrait:AmeliaHappy
Narrator: <i>The loss of life is simply a course of nature. In the grander scale of things, the flood allows us to farm without issue, scattering people across the land.</i>
Oren: Alas, when ideals burn bright with hope — some march their dreams onward, despite the current of the waves. #portrait:Oren 
Narrator: The next tapestry displays a stormy sea with tentacles rising out of the surface.
Oren: Deciding that it was his only chance to test his ideals against fate, Noah and Elio built a boat capable of entering the Dark Sea. #portrait:Oren
Oren: They recruited those who chose to support his hope. However, positive as he may seem, a seed of doubt settled in Noah. #portrait:Oren
Oren: Was he leading his friends onto a path of no return? #portrait:Oren
Narrator: The next tapestry shows Noah's ship, impressively detailed, sailing against the harsh winds.
Oren: When Noah's confidence wavered, his oldest and closest friend Elio grasped his hand, met his eyes, and— #portrait:Oren
Elder: Oren. #portrait:ElderAngry
Oren: …Told him that no matter what… He would stay by Noah's… side? #portrait:Oren
Narrator: Before Silas could chase him offstage, Oren quickly completes the story, flipping to the last tapestry.
Oren: Through rain and storm, the crew finally reached the Great Disaster's home! #portrait:Oren
Oren: Noah indeed spoke to the creature. God-like in presence and power, it loomed over him and his crew who waited from afar. #portrait:Oren
Oren: Yet in a brief moment, chaos unfolded! #portrait:Oren
Oren: The Great Disaster roared, striking Noah’s ship down, the mast snapped in half! #portrait:Oren
Oren: And while we may not know what began the outburst, what remains true is this.
Oren: A battle ensued, adventurers against creature — until finally, the Great Disaster sinks.
Narrator: The air is tense as the final illustration is shown. The kraken retreats underwater. 
Oren: …And so, there was no flood in Noah's time.
Noelle: (Dusk never told this story at all.) #portrait:NoelleSkeptical
Noelle: (Though, it makes sense… Even if Noah was from our home, we'd never know it was him who stopped the last flood.) #portrait:NoelleUm
Noelle: (Mother always said that it was just an oddity — a small miracle for laborers, but bringing a new challenge for farmers.) #portrait:NoelleUm
Noelle: (...Things will always balance out, in some way.) #portrait:NoelleUm
Narrator: That is at least one universal truth, in this world of waves upon waves. 
-> DONE